---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailSubject = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Partnership%20Ads%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20brand";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="Partnership Ads — The Complete Guide to Meta's Creator-Powered Ad Format | OKAD"
  description="Partnership Ads (formerly Branded Content Ads) let brands run paid ads using a creator's identity on Instagram and Facebook. This guide covers setup, permissions, formats, performance benchmarks, and how they compare to Spark Ads."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Open reference &middot; September 2026
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          Partnership Ads: <span class="text-accent">The Complete Guide</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          Meta Partnership Ads let brands run paid ads using a creator&rsquo;s identity on Instagram and Facebook. The creator&rsquo;s handle appears as the ad source &mdash; not the brand&rsquo;s. This guide covers everything brands and agencies need to know to set up, launch, and scale Partnership Ads.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailSubject} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Order Partnership Ads setup &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>Instagram &amp; Facebook</span>
          <span>Creator identity</span>
          <span>Native trust</span>
          <span>Reels &amp; Stories</span>
          <span>Meta Ads Manager</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works grid -->
    <section class="py-8">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-[22px] font-bold tracking-[-0.02em] text-center mb-5">How Partnership Ads Work</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F91D;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Creator Tags Brand</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Creator publishes content and tags the brand as a &ldquo;Paid Partnership.&rdquo; The post appears on the creator&rsquo;s profile with a partnership label.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F3AF;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Brand Promotes in Ads Manager</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Brand takes the tagged post and runs it as a paid ad through Meta Ads Manager. Full targeting, bidding, and optimization controls apply.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4F1;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Ad Runs Under Creator&rsquo;s Handle</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">The ad appears in feeds with the creator&rsquo;s name and profile photo &mdash; not the brand&rsquo;s. Users see creator content, not a brand ad.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "Overview", href: "#overview" },
            { num: "02", text: "How Partnership Ads Work", href: "#how-it-works" },
            { num: "03", text: "Requirements & Permissions", href: "#requirements" },
            { num: "04", text: "Formats & Placements", href: "#formats" },
            { num: "05", text: "Partnership Ads vs Standard Ads", href: "#vs-standard" },
            { num: "06", text: "Partnership Ads vs Spark Ads", href: "#vs-spark" },
            { num: "07", text: "Best Practices", href: "#best-practices" },
            { num: "08", text: "Measurement & Optimization", href: "#measurement" },
            { num: "09", text: "Glossary", href: "#glossary" },
            { num: "10", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Overview -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="overview">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Overview</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">What are Partnership Ads?</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Meta&rsquo;s ad format that lets brands run paid ads using a creator&rsquo;s identity</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Formerly called Branded Content Ads &mdash; rebranded to Partnership Ads in 2023</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The ad appears under the creator&rsquo;s handle, not the brand&rsquo;s account</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Partnership Ads</strong> are Meta&rsquo;s creator-powered ad format for Instagram and Facebook. They allow a brand to take content that a creator has tagged as a &ldquo;Paid Partnership&rdquo; and promote it as a paid ad through Meta Ads Manager. The resulting ad appears in users&rsquo; feeds with the creator&rsquo;s name, profile photo, and handle &mdash; not the brand&rsquo;s. This creates a native trust signal that standard brand ads cannot replicate.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Meta originally launched this format as <strong>Branded Content Ads</strong> in 2019. The rebrand to &ldquo;Partnership Ads&rdquo; happened in mid-2023 as part of a broader update to Meta&rsquo;s creator monetization tools. The underlying mechanics remain the same: a creator publishes content with a paid partnership tag, and the brand promotes it as an ad. But the expanded toolkit now includes more granular permissions, ad-level identity controls, and improved reporting for both parties.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For brands running <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing</a> programs, Partnership Ads are a critical format. They let you amplify top-performing creator content &mdash; whether from <a href="/canvas-ugc" class="text-accent hover:underline">Canvas UGC</a> programs, influencer collaborations, or dedicated <a href="/branded-content-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">branded content</a> campaigns &mdash; as paid media with the creator&rsquo;s identity intact. The result is ads that feel like organic creator posts, not polished brand advertising.</p>
        <ul class="list-none p-0 space-y-2">
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Platform:</strong> Instagram and Facebook (Meta ecosystem)</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Ad identity:</strong> creator&rsquo;s handle appears as the ad source</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Setup:</strong> creator tags brand, brand promotes from Ads Manager</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Formats:</strong> Reels, Stories, Feed posts, Carousel (Instagram and Facebook)</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">TikTok equivalent:</strong> <a href="/spark-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">Spark Ads</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 How Partnership Ads Work -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="how-it-works">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Setup Process</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How Partnership Ads work, step by step</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Creator publishes content with a &ldquo;Paid Partnership&rdquo; tag</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand approves the tag and gets access to promote the post</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand creates the ad in Meta Ads Manager using the partnership post</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The Partnership Ads workflow involves coordination between the creator and the brand. Unlike standard ads where the brand uploads creative directly, Partnership Ads require the creator to initiate the process by publishing content and granting the brand permission to promote it. Here is the full sequence.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The setup process</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Creator switches to a Professional account.</strong> Partnership Ads require the creator to have an Instagram Professional account (Creator or Business). Personal accounts cannot tag paid partnerships.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Brand adds creator as an approved partner.</strong> In Meta Business Suite, the brand navigates to Settings &rarr; Branded Content and adds the creator&rsquo;s Instagram handle as an approved partner. This allows the creator to tag the brand in paid partnership posts.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Creator publishes content with the Paid Partnership tag.</strong> When creating a post, Reel, or Story, the creator enables &ldquo;Add paid partnership label&rdquo; in advanced settings and selects the brand as the partner. The post goes live with a &ldquo;Paid partnership with [Brand]&rdquo; label.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Creator grants ad creation access.</strong> The creator toggles &ldquo;Allow brand partner to promote&rdquo; when adding the partnership label. This gives the brand permission to use the post as an ad creative in Ads Manager.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Brand creates the ad in Meta Ads Manager.</strong> The brand opens Ads Manager, creates a new campaign, and selects the partnership post as the ad creative. The brand applies its own targeting, budget, bidding strategy, and optimization goals.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Ad runs under the creator&rsquo;s identity.</strong> The promoted post appears in users&rsquo; feeds with the creator&rsquo;s name, handle, and profile photo. A &ldquo;Sponsored&rdquo; label and the &ldquo;Paid partnership&rdquo; tag are visible, but the primary identity is the creator &mdash; not the brand.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What users see</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When a Partnership Ad appears in someone&rsquo;s feed, the creator&rsquo;s profile photo and handle are displayed at the top of the post &mdash; exactly as if the creator had posted it organically. Below the handle, a small &ldquo;Paid partnership with [Brand]&rdquo; label appears, along with the standard &ldquo;Sponsored&rdquo; tag. The visual hierarchy prioritizes the creator&rsquo;s identity, which is why Partnership Ads consistently outperform standard brand ads on trust and engagement metrics.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This is the fundamental advantage. Users scroll past brand ads reflexively. A post that appears to come from a real creator &mdash; someone they might follow or whose content they consume &mdash; gets more attention, more engagement, and more trust. The ad is still clearly labeled as sponsored, but the psychological framing is &ldquo;creator content&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;brand advertisement.&rdquo;</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 Requirements & Permissions -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="requirements">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Requirements</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Requirements and permissions</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Creator needs an Instagram Professional account (Creator or Business)</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand must add creator as an approved partner in Meta Business Suite</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Creator must explicitly grant &ldquo;Allow brand partner to promote&rdquo; permission</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Creator requirements</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The creator must have a <strong>Professional account</strong> on Instagram &mdash; either the Creator or Business account type. Personal accounts do not have access to the paid partnership tag. Switching to a Professional account is free and takes about two minutes in Instagram settings. Once switched, the creator gains access to branded content tools, insights, and the ability to tag commercial partners.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The creator must also comply with Meta&rsquo;s <strong>Branded Content Policies</strong>. These prohibit misleading claims, require transparent disclosure, and restrict certain product categories (tobacco, weapons, adult content). Creators who violate these policies risk losing access to branded content tools entirely.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Brand requirements</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The brand needs an active <strong>Meta Business Suite</strong> (formerly Business Manager) account with a connected Instagram Business account and an Ads Manager account. The brand must add the creator as an <strong>approved branded content partner</strong> in Business Suite settings. This creates a two-way connection: the creator can tag the brand, and the brand can promote the tagged content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Brands also need to ensure their Ads Manager account is in good standing &mdash; no policy violations, active payment method, and verified business identity. Partnership Ads use the same campaign infrastructure as standard Meta ads, so all standard Ads Manager requirements apply.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Permission levels</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Permission</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Who grants it</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What it allows</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Paid Partnership tag</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Creator (per post)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Creator labels the post as a paid partnership with the brand</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Approved partner status</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Brand (in Business Suite)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Creator can tag this brand without manual approval per post</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Ad creation access</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Creator (per post toggle)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Brand can use this specific post as an ad creative in Ads Manager</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Ad-level creator identity</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Automatic (Meta)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">The ad displays the creator&rsquo;s name, handle, and profile photo</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The key distinction is that ad creation access is <strong>opt-in per post</strong>. The creator must explicitly allow the brand to promote each piece of content. This protects creators from having their identity used in ads they did not approve. It also means brands need to coordinate with creators to ensure the permission toggle is enabled before launching campaigns.</p>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-6 my-6 flex gap-4 items-start flex-col sm:flex-row">
          <div class="w-11 h-11 rounded-[10px] bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.12)] flex items-center justify-center text-[20px] shrink-0">&#9889;</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-[16px] font-semibold mb-1"><a href={emailSubject} class="text-ink hover:text-accent transition-colors no-underline">OKAD handles creator permissions and partnership setup</a></h4>
            <p class="text-[14px] text-muted m-0 leading-[1.5]">We manage the entire onboarding flow &mdash; from switching creators to Professional accounts, to setting up approved partner connections, to ensuring ad creation access is granted on every post. No back-and-forth for your team.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Formats & Placements -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="formats">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Formats</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Formats and placements</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Partnership Ads work across Reels, Stories, Feed, and Carousel on both Instagram and Facebook</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Instagram Reels are the highest-performing placement for most brands</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Each format has different specs, best practices, and performance profiles</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Partnership Ads support every major content format across both Instagram and Facebook. The format of the original creator post determines where it can be placed as an ad. A Reel can be promoted as a Reels ad. A Feed photo can run in Feed placements. This flexibility lets brands test different content types while maintaining the creator identity advantage.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Format</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Platform</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Best for</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Specs</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Reels</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Instagram, Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Top-funnel awareness, engagement, reach</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">9:16, up to 90s</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Stories</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Instagram, Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Direct response, swipe-up CTAs, urgency</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">9:16, up to 60s</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Feed (video)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Instagram, Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Longer storytelling, product demos</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">1:1 or 4:5, up to 60min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Feed (image)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Instagram, Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Product shots, lifestyle, static promos</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">1:1 or 4:5</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Carousel</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Instagram, Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Multi-product, tutorials, before/after</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">1:1, up to 10 cards</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Instagram Reels: the dominant format</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For most brands in 2026, Instagram Reels is the primary format for Partnership Ads. Reels are full-screen, sound-on, and algorithmically distributed to users beyond the creator&rsquo;s follower base. When promoted as a Partnership Ad, a Reel retains its native feel &mdash; users encounter it in the Reels feed alongside organic content. The creator identity is displayed at the top, and the ad integrates seamlessly into the browsing experience.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The performance advantage of Reels over static feed placements is significant. Reels Partnership Ads typically deliver 20&ndash;40% lower cost-per-engagement compared to feed image ads, and the format naturally supports the kind of authentic, creator-led storytelling that Partnership Ads are designed for. If you are running Partnership Ads for the first time, start with Reels.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Stories: the direct-response workhorse</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Stories Partnership Ads are ideal for direct-response campaigns. The full-screen format combined with swipe-up (or link sticker) CTAs creates a short path from attention to action. Stories feel intimate and time-sensitive, which makes them effective for limited-time offers, product launches, and app install campaigns. The trade-off is that Stories have a shorter content lifespan and lower organic reach than Reels.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Facebook placements</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Partnership Ads are not limited to Instagram. Facebook Feed, Facebook Reels, and Facebook Stories all support the format. For brands targeting demographics that skew older or are more active on Facebook (particularly in markets outside the US), Facebook placements can deliver significantly lower CPMs. The creative format and partnership mechanics work identically across both platforms.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Partnership Ads vs Standard Ads -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="vs-standard">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Comparison</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Partnership Ads vs standard brand ads</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Partnership Ads outperform standard ads on trust, engagement, and conversion</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The creator identity is the key differentiator</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Standard ads still have advantages in speed and creative control</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The core question for most brands is straightforward: why use Partnership Ads instead of running standard ads from the brand&rsquo;s own account? The answer comes down to identity and trust. When an ad appears under a creator&rsquo;s handle, users process it differently than when it appears under a brand&rsquo;s logo. The creator identity triggers a &ldquo;content&rdquo; frame rather than an &ldquo;advertisement&rdquo; frame, which leads to measurably different performance.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Metric</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Partnership Ads</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Standard Brand Ads</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Ad identity</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Creator&rsquo;s handle and profile photo</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Brand&rsquo;s logo and account name</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Trust signal</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">High &mdash; perceived as creator content</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Lower &mdash; immediately recognized as brand ad</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Engagement rate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Typically 2&ndash;3x higher than standard ads</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Baseline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CTR (click-through rate)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">15&ndash;50% higher on average</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Baseline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Comparable or slightly higher</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Baseline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Conversion rate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">10&ndash;25% higher with strong creator match</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Baseline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Creative control</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Requires creator coordination</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Full control &mdash; brand uploads directly</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Speed to launch</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Slower &mdash; depends on creator posting and permissions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Fast &mdash; brand creates ad immediately</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Best for</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Trust-driven campaigns, top-funnel, social proof</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Retargeting, promotions, brand-controlled messaging</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The performance gap is most pronounced at the top of the funnel &mdash; awareness and consideration campaigns where trust is the primary barrier. Users who have never heard of a brand are significantly more likely to engage with content that appears to come from a creator they relate to than from an unfamiliar brand account. At the bottom of the funnel (retargeting, cart abandonment), the gap narrows because users already know the brand.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The trade-off is operational complexity. Standard ads give the brand complete control &mdash; upload creative, set targeting, launch. Partnership Ads require coordination with the creator, dependency on their posting schedule, and a permission-granting workflow. For brands running <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing</a> at scale, this operational overhead is worth the performance gain, but it requires either dedicated internal resources or an agency partner to manage.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

